 			<p>“Please, Madam Mother, could you lend me my wife for half an hour? The luggage has come, and I’ve been making hay of Amy’s Paris finery, trying to find some things I want,” said Laurie, coming in the next day to find <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mrs.</abbr> Laurence sitting in her mother’s lap, as if being made “the baby” again.</p>
 			<p>“Certainly. Go, dear; I forget that you have any home but this,” and <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mrs.</abbr> March pressed the white hand that wore the wedding-ring, as if asking pardon for her maternal covetousness.</p>
